le family  had died  you'd meet children who'd lost all their  limbs and actually it was there was a  young  kid called darwood and darwin had lost  his legs and his arm he'd been injured  like i had  and he was so resilient and and positive  and  every time i passed him he'd always  smile look at me and i chat to his  mother  and after a few days it was actually his  mother said to me you know why are you  not taking photographs  i could take a photograph of somebody's  suffering i could take a photograph of  this child  injured but whatever photograph i took  that day  tomorrow more we're gonna die more we're  gonna be  injured and she looked at me and she was  kind of stern  and she pointed it downward and she said  when a child is injured like this  the whole world should see it and it  kind of snapped me back  into doing my job  i was described the stories as like  little scars every single story affects  you and and takes you home yeah one  story that that probably is the most  important story to me is  of a woman called hollud uh hulud had  been at home in syria  during the civil war there and a sniper  shot her in the neck  she was actually in the garden tending  to some vegetables and she fell on one  of her children  she got emergency treatment and then  they took her to lebanon to the bekhar  valley where i met her  if you imagine a woman a tetraplegic  woman paralyzed from the neck down  lying in a makeshift tent she had no  medical support apart from her husband  jamal who was her full-time carer  anyway i photographed her and her  husband  and this was soon after my own injury  and so i was just coming back  returning to work so it was a really  important story for me i came back and  the story was published  kind of appeared around the world and  and i must admit  i kind of moved on from it and then a  couple of years later i was back in  lebanon and i tracked down some of the  families that i'd met  before including khalood and and jamal  and i remember that phone call so  clearly to this day um  it was jamal and said over here you're  back in lebanon we'd like to see you  and i said well where are you and he  said we're in the same place you last  saw us  and stupidly i hadn't even thought to  look for them there i just assumed there  was no  way that this woman could still be  living in that same shitty situation  surely the whole point of what i do is  it changes somebody's life it does  something  can you imagine this tiny little room  there's no windows in the two years  since i've seen her she had not moved  from that bed just staring at the same  bit of ceiling  and yet it's a place full of love and  laughter and happiness and the kids are  always laughing and  joking and the husband jamal when we go  to the little kitchen on the side he  always whispers to me his greatest fear  is that she doesn't love him as much as  he loves her  but khalood is like a she's like an  angel she just smiled and she's like  look we knew you'd come back it's fine  and i thought well all i can do is is  focus on on doing my job better this  time so i spent the next week  documenting their daily life and i like  to take photographs back with me so that  i can i can share  the way that i've represented people and  on the last day before i left i was  thinking should i give this photograph  back to  cloud and jamal that i'd taken two years  before because nothing had changed  literally the same photograph was in  front of me but i thought now i have to  and i handed this photograph  to khalood but before i did i said to  her when i took this photograph  i did not take a photograph of a refugee  i did not take a photograph of a  disabled woman when i took this  photograph i took a photograph of a  couple who are so  in love with each other and actually  this is a photograph of love  because she made me realize i'm not a  war photographer  i document love you will never see a  photograph i take of a gun  of violence or any act like that what i  photograph  is those moments of intimacies a  grandmother brushing granddaughter's  hair  father on the ground doing maths with  his kids a couple  like khalid and jamal holding hands and  what i find in the worst places in the  world  is incredible humanity and i find love  you know somebody asked me once do you  think you can change the world with your  photographs  and i said no but maybe i can inspire  the person that can  [Music]  you
 

